course: adult-life
lesson: 47
title: Viral rumours, cancellations, fakes and apologies
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some stories about famous people race round the internet in an hour; the correction, if it ever comes, crawls along afterwards in a tiny box on page 42.
explain: To sort a viral story, ask: who says so, did the person themselves say it, is a picture edited or taken out of its story, and is someone trying to sell me something or trick me?
explain: Vivid example: in Brisbane, little Jayden runs in: "Mum! That YouTuber's giving away free phones, you just type your password in!" Mum takes the tablet off him: "That's a scam with his face on it, love. Free prizes that want your password are traps."
explain: Some public fights are a show, like boxers' trash talk before a match; some are real hurt, like thousands of cruel messages to one writer. And when the law decides a story was a lie, the magazine has to pay.
fact: Hopes are not news: "a special guest" at assembly does not mean the singer everybody wants.
fact: Corrections are usually much smaller than the false stories they correct, so be slow to believe a shocking first story.
fact: Many online photos are filtered or edited; comparing your real face to an edited picture is unfair on yourself.
fact: Every age had its gossip papers; the screens are new, the gossip is not.
fact: Reality shows plan scenes and edit them into a story; the people in them are still real and can be hated for an edited version of themselves.
fact: Famous faces are used in scams; a real giveaway never asks for your password.
fact: Boxers' insults at a press conference are "trash talk", a show to sell tickets; that does not make insults okay at school.
fact: Trolling means sending many cruel messages online; you can disagree strongly with someone without abusing them.
fact: When someone gives a reason, like being ill, swapping it for a meaner guess is cruel.
fact: "Charged" with a crime is not the same as "guilty"; the court decides, so wait for it.
fact: Printing harmful lies about a person can be against the law; it is called defamation, and a court can make the paper pay the person money for the harm.
fact: Famous people still buy chocolate, rewear dresses and get haircuts; none of that is a scandal.
fact: Sharing where a famous person lives is wrong; being a fan gives nobody that right.
fact: Young famous people, like teenage TV actors, deserve even more privacy, because they are still children.
exercise: A kid says, "That singer's coming to our school, my friend said!" The school only said "a special guest". What is it? | A hopeful guess, not news | A fact the school has confirmed | A secret the head teacher told | A rumour the singer started herself
exercise: A magazine prints a small apology on page 42 for a front-page story about an actress. What does this teach? | Corrections are much smaller than the lies | Page 42 is the most important page | The actress wrote the first story about herself to get attention | Apologies always go on the front page
exercise: Big sister: "Her 'natural' photos are filtered!" What should you remember? | Many photos are edited; don't compare your real face | Every single thing she does is completely fake, even her name | Filters are against the law in photos | Real skin always looks like the photos
exercise: Grandad: "In my day famous people had dignity." What is fairer? | Every age had gossip; only the screens changed | Nobody was ever gossiped about before mobile phones | Famous people didn't exist in his day | Grandad never read a newspaper at all
exercise: Dad says a reality show films its arguments three times. Which is closest to the truth? | Many scenes are planned and edited into a story | Every word on the show is a written script | Nothing on reality TV is ever planned | The cast are all actors pretending to be ordinary people
exercise: A video says a YouTuber gives free phones if you type your password. What is it? | A scam using his face | A real giveaway for his fans | A competition run by the bank | A prize that only works once
exercise: Nan: "Her own dad sold a story about her to the papers!" How should we see it? | A sad family hurt, not entertainment | A fun story to laugh at with friends | Proof the singer is a bad daughter | Normal, every dad does that
exercise: A boxer says he'll knock the other out in one round. What is this mostly? | Trash talk, a show to sell tickets | A real threat the police must stop | A promise he has to keep by law | A secret plan he let slip by accident
exercise: An author gets thousands of horrible messages for what she said. What is the right way to disagree? | Say so strongly, without cruel messages | Send even more messages than the others | Never disagree with anyone at all | Find out where she lives and tell people
exercise: A singer cancels a concert saying she's ill. Fans say, "Nobody bought tickets." What is that? | A meaner guess swapped for her real reason | The real reason, which she hid from everyone at first | A fact from the ticket company | A kind way to explain the news
exercise: The BBC says a company boss has been charged with fraud. What does "charged" mean? | Accused in court, not yet proved guilty | Found guilty by the judge and sent to prison | Let off without any trial at all | Made to pay a parking fine
exercise: A court orders a magazine to pay an actress for printing lies about her. What is this called? | Defamation | Copyright | Privacy filter | Trash talk
exercise: A photo shows an actor buying lots of chocolate. A kid says, "Greedy!" What's fairer? | Famous people still shop; it could be for anything | He must eat all of it in one night | Actors aren't allowed to buy chocolate in their contracts | It proves he will be ill tomorrow
exercise: Nan: "That young singer's gone off the rails, she's cut all her hair off!" What is confirmed? | Only that she has short hair now | That something is wrong with her mind | That her family are very worried | That she was told to cut it for a film
exercise: An actress wears the same dress to two events. "Fashion fail!" say the kids. Which is true? | Rewearing clothes is sensible, not a scandal | Famous people must wear a new dress each time | It means she has run out of money | Dresses fall apart if worn twice
exercise: Fans share a pop star's home address and gather outside. Why is that wrong? | A home address is private, famous or not | It's fine if they only stand there quietly and wait | Pop stars like fans at their door | It's only wrong if it's raining
exercise: Big brother: "Those two from the TV shows are dating, new power couple!" They were only at the same party. Why be extra careful here? | They are teenagers, still children themselves | Teen actors are never allowed to date anyone by law | The party was secret and illegal | Their fans will be bored by the news
test: Dad, fuming: "Manager said the ref was blind! He'll get fined, rightly so." Who often gets hurt after such words? | The referee, who gets abuse from fans | The manager, who is sent to prison | The ball boys, who lose their jobs | Nobody, it is just a football chat
test: Mum, giggling: "Newsreader said the wrong word on live telly and the whole internet's having a laugh." When does it stop being okay? | When one little slip is mocked for weeks | It is never okay to smile at a slip | Only if the newsreader was very famous | It is always okay, she's on the telly
test: Auntie Bev: "That famous family are all at war over their dad's will, I read." Why should we be careful? | They are grieving, and money in families is private | Wills are always printed in the newspapers for anyone to read | All rich families fight over wills | The dad is still alive and fine
test: Auntie Jen, laughing: "She demands white flowers AND a puppy at every concert, total diva!" The list came from an unnamed source. What is likely? | The funny bits like the puppy are made up | Every word of the list is a fact | The singer wrote the list herself online | Puppies are sent to every concert hall
test: Your mate: "he's a millionaire, lives in a mansion in Dubai, my mate said." The YouTuber filmed some videos in Dubai. What do videos show? | A place, not his bank balance | His bank statement for the year | Where he lives every single day | How many millions he owns
test: Auntie Bev: "That actor's a terrible dad, never sees his kids." The magazine only said he was filming abroad. What is the problem? | Judging his parenting from a work schedule | He should never have gone to work | Magazines know families very well | His kids must have rung up the magazine and told them everything
test: Big sis texts: "free iphone from that youtuber!!! just put ur password in, quick b4 it ends" What do you do? | Don't type it, it's a scam; tell a grown-up | Type it fast before the time runs out | Send it to your friends so they win too | Type a password that isn't quite yours
test: Your little brother: "Reality TV is real, that's why it's called reality!" Put him right. | Scenes are planned and edited, though the people are real | He's right, every moment is just as it happened, nothing is cut | It's all actors and none of it is real | It's called reality because it's on at night
test: Mum reads: "Company boss charged with fraud." Your cousin says, "So he's a crook then." Put him right. | Charged isn't guilty; the court decides | Yes, being charged means he did it | No, bosses can never be charged | Yes, the BBC has decided it already
test: Mum shows you a tiny box on page 42: "Correction: our story about the actress was untrue." The lie was on the front page. Why does it matter? | Most readers saw the lie but never the correction | The correction will be on the front page in big letters next week | The actress wanted it on page 42 | Corrections are bigger than the stories
test: Your mate: "she's not ill, she's just lying cos no one bought tickets lol." What's the kind and fair reply? | She said she's ill; don't swap it for a meaner guess | You're right, singers always lie about that | Let's post that on her page | She deserves it for cancelling
exam: Determine the legal term which denotes the publication of false statements injurious to the reputation of a person. | Defamation | Correction | Advertisement | Satire
exam: Identify the principal distinction between a person who has been charged with an offence and a person who has been convicted of it. | The former has been accused, whereas the latter has been found guilty | The former has been found guilty, whereas the latter has merely been accused | The former has paid a fine, whereas the latter has been released | There exists no distinction between the two
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning invitations to enter a password in order to obtain a free telephone from a celebrated person. | Such invitations are fraudulent and must not be answered | Such invitations are genuine where the celebrity's face appears | Such invitations may be answered if the offer is time-limited | Such invitations are lawful competitions conducted by banks
exam: Ascertain the conclusion which may properly be drawn from the observation that a correction is printed upon a minor page whereas the original false report occupied the front page. | Fewer readers will learn of the correction than of the falsehood | The correction is of greater importance than the original | The original report has been proven true thereby | The subject of the report requested the arrangement
